Using Apache Kafka for AI and ML
Apache Kafka is not just used for big data processing. It has several uses in the world of artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML).

Data ingestion for model training:
When training data needs to be collected from distributed sources like IoT devices, machine logs and remote applications, a Kafka pipeline can be used 
to acquire and move data for model training. During this process, data can undergo feature engineering, too, when combined with big data frameworks like 
Apache Kafka. 

Real-time model inference:
Apache Kafka can be used as a queue for asynchronous real time inference. It can deliver live data streams to ML models to make real-time predictions. 
The data stream can either originate from other applications or from users. The ML model service subscribes to a Kafka request queue. As new records appear 
in the queue, the ML model service reads them, does predictions, and pushes the results to a response queue. Clients then read the results of this response queue.
Having a queue in the middle throttles requests to the ML model service so that service is not overwhelmed by sudden request spikes.

Monitoring and metrics:
As the ML models do predictions, data needs to be collected about the inputs to the model, outputs from the model, latency, and resource utilization. 
Such telemetry can be captured and published to a Kafka queue. Using a queue helps the ML model service to publish and move on with other tasks. 
Metrics collection and aggregation applications can then work off the Kafka queue to process data and store results.
